/**
 * One engine under measurement, in its own compilation unit.
 *
 * WHY THIS EXISTS AT ALL. Bench.java.template has to compile against every release in the version
 * bisect, back to 0.3.0.2. The fork ships engines those releases never had - Mutiny and the proxy
 * exist in no published release, and the proxy is not even on Maven Central. A single template that
 * imported them would fail to compile for ten of the eleven versions in PC_VERSIONS, which is to say
 * it would delete the version bisect in order to add an engine arm.
 *
 * So the shared harness names no engine type. It resolves an arm by class name (see
 * Bench#armFor) and run-bisect.sh compiles into each arm directory only the arm files the modes
 * being swept need, with only the Maven artifacts those arms need. Adding an engine is one new file
 * here plus one line in run-bisect.sh's arm table; it costs the other arms nothing and it cannot
 * break the bisect.
 *
 * WHAT AN ARM MAY AND MAY NOT DO. It runs the engine and returns the measured window. It must not
 * define its own counters, its own consumer properties, its own options builder or its own callee -
 * those live in Bench and are shared precisely so that two arms differ by the engine and by nothing
 * else. An arm that reimplements one of them is measuring a different experiment.
 */
public interface BenchArm {

    /**
     * Runs one measurement. The callee (if this arm uses the shared HTTP one) is already started.
     *
     * @return elapsed wall-clock milliseconds of the measured window - started immediately before the
     *         engine is handed its user function, ended when the expected record count has completed.
     *         The window includes the consumer-group join for every arm, which is the convention every
     *         committed results file was taken under; see
     *         docs/inflight/perf-platform-threads-are-the-ceiling.md on why that matters when reading
     *         mean in-flight back out of a throughput number.
     */
    long run(String bootstrap, String topic, int expected, int delayMs, int concurrency, int bufferSize)
            throws Exception;

    /**
     * Whether Bench should start the shared callee (WireMock, the async stub under BENCH_ASYNC_STUB,
     * or the bare timer under BENCH_TIMER_CALLEE) before {@link #run}.
     * <p>
     * True for every engine whose simulated work is an HTTP request, which is all of them except the
     * proxy: the proxy's callee is a connected worker on the far side of the dispatch protocol, not an
     * HTTP server, so its arm builds its own and honours {@link Bench#asyncCallee()} itself. That is
     * the same axis under a different transport, deliberately - so the "does the callee hold a thread"
     * question is asked identically of every engine.
     */
    default boolean usesSharedCallee() {
        return true;
    }
}
